// Merkle-committed rank lookup table (Issue #222).
//
// This replaces the inline, constraint-heavy C(7,5) hand evaluator used inside
// the showdown circuit with a table lookup: a hand's rank score is verified to
// be a member of a Merkle tree whose root is committed as a public input, and
// the verified score is then used for winner/tie selection.
//
// The table contains every distinct 5-card hand score that the reference
// evaluator (`cards::evaluate_hand_rank`) can produce. It is generated offline
// from that evaluator, so the scores it returns for any hand are identical to
// the inline evaluator's, preserving showdown output for game_type == 0 while
// removing the per-player 21-combination enumeration.
//
//   2,847 distinct achievable scores, stored in a 4,096-leaf tree (depth 12),
//   padded with zeros (no achievable score is zero, so padding cannot collide).
//
// ===== Soundness note =====
// An inclusion proof here shows that `score` is a *valid* hand rank (a member
// of the committed table). It does not, on its own, bind `score` to a
// particular set of hole/board cards; binding the score to the player's actual
// cards is the circuit's responsibility (see showdown_valid). The table and
// its root are deterministic and can be reproduced by any prover.

use crate::merkle;

pub global RANK_TABLE_LEAVES: u32 = 4096;
pub global RANK_TABLE_DEPTH: u32 = 12;

// Sorted list of the 2,847 distinct achievable 5-card scores, padded to a
// 4,096-leaf tree with zeros. Generated offline from the reference evaluator.
